A||||3||GodSend||Problems with winter beater...||||11||
Z||000000||GodSend||10-21-2000||11:00 PM||||  My fiero goes away for the winter in under a month and my winter beater is giving me problems.  Although its not a fiero could you guys help me out?<P>  I have a Suzuki Sidekick.  Manual tranny.  This happened only ONCE in the summer and ONCE yesturday.<P>  It seems that after extreme stop and go traffic (10+ minutes of creeping in a traffic jam) the shifter gets harder and harder to put in gear untill finally you can not at all.<P>  What happens is I push the clutch petal all teh way down, but you cannot move the stick shifter into any gear.. no matter how hard you push the clutch down or push the stick.  As SOON as you turn the car off, no problems, start it back up... problems.  <P>  If you let it cool down for about 10 minutes it works fine (except if you have to get back into the traffic jam, then it acts up).<P>  This has happened on both occasions I was stuck in traffic (as I do not drive it very often at all).  The clutch seems PERFECT on all other occasions.. drives perfect.  ANy suggestions guys?..||||reg||11
Z||000001||MrPBody||10-21-2000||11:31 PM||||Sounds like the clutch is not releasing completely.  Why?  Dunno.  Most likely causes (that I can think of) are:<P>1) clutch hydraulics (master and/or slave cylinder)<P>2) oily clutch disk, probably caused by engine or transmission leak<P>3) dragging clutch pilot bearing<P>4) mechanical problem in the clutch release linkage, such as bent release fork (which probably would cause a constant -- not occasional -- problem).<P>I'm pulling this out of the air, as I know nothing about your car.  Good luck!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-21-2000||11:43 PM||||<B>"after extreme stop and go traffic (10+ minutes of creeping in a traffic jam)"</B><P>LOL! 10 minutes of stop an go is a traffic jam??!! A "major" one! Man, I just wish I lived in a place like that. It was just yesterday morning on my way to school it took me 25 minutes to go slightly over a mile. An that is usually typical unless I take the "short cut" through the ghetto and across a parking lot the wrong direction. Then I can make it in under 20.<P>Sounds hydralic to me, most likely culprit would be the master cylinder. If you decide to rebuild it, remember, a rebuild(IMO) has at most a 50/50 shot at working correctly. I would suggest a new OEM part, unless SideKick parts are real expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GodSend||10-22-2000||12:12 AM||||  Well... 10 minutes being the min. time.  I mentioned it only to illustrate it does not happen at traffic lights.<P>  Just today I was stuck for an hour and half on the 410 in Toronto.  ||||reg||1
